EXT. ROCK LEDGE ABOVE A FROZEN RIVER, YUKON - DUSK

A narrow shelf of granite thirty feet over the ice. Spruce stunted by wind. Snow in drifts. A fire of three damp sticks, hardly burning.

OLE BRANDVOLD-ACHEBE (60s, trapper, a beard crusted white, wolf-fur hood) crouches over the fire with a tin cup. Next to him, wrapped in a wool blanket, sits PEARL TSOSIE-HARLOW (20s, surveyor, red nose, a compass hung on a cord). Between them, a pot of water that refuses to boil.

PEARL
Is it going to boil?

OLE
Eventually.

PEARL
When?

OLE
When it's ready.

She tucks her blanket tighter. Ole prods the fire with a stick.

PEARL
(to herself)
Three days.

OLE
What's that?

PEARL
Nothing. Counting.

OLE
Counting what?

PEARL
Days since we left the cabin.

OLE
Four.

PEARL
It was three.

OLE
It was four. You lost one in the whiteout.

PEARL
I don't remember a whiteout.

OLE
You wouldn't. You were asleep.

She looks at him, then at the river below. A pale glow lingers on the western ridge. The wind lifts and drops.

PEARL
Is there food?

OLE
One tin.

PEARL
What's in it?

OLE
Beans.

PEARL
Good.

OLE
Maybe.

PEARL
(to the fire)
They'll come looking.

OLE
Who?

PEARL
The party. The survey team.

OLE
They don't know where we are.

PEARL
They know the river.

OLE
The river's twelve hundred miles long, girl.

PEARL
They know which bend.

OLE
How?

PEARL
I told them.

Ole lowers the stick. Looks at her.

OLE
You told them.

PEARL
In the letter. The one you posted.

OLE
I posted it at the last trading station.

PEARL
Then it's a week ahead of us.

OLE
(slowly)
So somebody knows.

PEARL
Somebody does.

He puts a fresh stick in the fire. It catches. The pot begins, at last, to murmur.

OLE
(to the camp, quietly)
We'll hold here.

PEARL
For how long?

OLE
Till somebody comes.

The first bubble breaks.
